<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  macOS entitlements. release.sh signs with this file simply because it exists here —
  that presence is the switch, so the sandbox needs no flag and no configuration.

  The game asks for nothing but the sandbox itself: it carries its own assets through
  //go:embed and writes only to the per-user config directory, which the sandbox
  redirects into the app container on its own. No network, no file pickers, no
  microphone. That is also what makes it Mac App Store material without further work.

  The one flag that stops working under the sandbox is -dir, which points the game at a
  game-assets tree outside the container; that flag is a development tool, and a
  sandboxed build runs from the embedded content instead.

  Gamepad support may need com.apple.security.device.bluetooth for wireless controllers
  once it lands — to be confirmed against a real controller, not guessed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
